e","related_refs":[],"schema":"bridge.process-observation.v1","statement":"At formal sequence 939, artifact 52653dd4cd45eece6e9996c5d543a975bf858d8e66db29a2a7f7db30cc96f294 had formal status accepted.","subject_ref":"52653dd4cd45eece6e9996c5d543a975bf858d8e66db29a2a7f7db30cc96f294"},{"formal_seq":939,"id":"sha256:e2a31b4da6bc6e1ef4ccb880d9cee7259f6ec92734efd9f647f63c6724b79716","observation_kind":"acceptance","related_refs":[],"schema":"bridge.process-observation.v1","statement":"At formal sequence 939, artifact 1de19c54e39e3135f9db3b07972a95d4b025bc0cc1d7362962bdd403c265328d had formal status accepted.","subject_ref":"1de19c54e39e3135f9db3b07972a95d4b025bc0cc1d7362962bdd403c265328d"},{"formal_seq":939,"id":"sha256:7cfc7a48e362b03d09ac37f5c28a7d6aebed606b9b7832842c2d367d91f2d7ad","observation_kind":"acceptance","related_refs":[],"schema":"bridge.process-observation.v1","statement":"At formal sequence 939, artifact f31d6660128c1ec936af55e0c921d58ac586218770bc1d4834be4e48e37fc279 had formal status accepted.","subject_ref":"f31d6660128c1ec936af55e0c921d58ac586218770bc1d4834be4e48e37fc279"}],"retrieval_receipt_ref":"sha256:14e2706e31303eaeb9678004ec9591d2d6b80d199ad2680c801481622bded5c2","schema":"bridge.catalog.v3"},"composition_request":{"desired_length_chars":16384,"formatting_profile":"plain","maximum_sections":4,"output_target":"answer"},"evidence_budget_chars":24000,"evidence_pack":{"argued_refutations":[],"catalog_contract":"bridge.catalog.v3","catalog_items":[{"excerpt":"[d1c3422e3d18] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Resumption is safe if and only if the last recorded event in the log is well-formed and complete, and the causal preconditions (program, inputs, model-state checkpoint) are demonstrably unchanged at resumption time. The append-only identity is preserved because new events extend the same causal chain without rewriting any prior event.\nMECHANISM: An event log maintains append-only identity through a causal chain: each event references its predecessor. Token-budget exhaustion is an external interrupt, not a logical termination. If the interrupt fell between complete events (not mid-write), the last event's hash serves as a resume cursor. The resumption process reads this cursor, verifies the program/input/model fingerprint matches, and appends new events that reference the last committed event as their causal parent. No rewrite occurs, so append-onl","handle":"A1","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"d1c3422e3d18993ac034bc5bbd3b90f90d3294847aca130493c4c8c6272330cb"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact d1c3422e3d18993ac034bc5bbd3b90f90d3294847aca130493c4c8c6272330cb had formal status accepted.","handle":"P1","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:b5ff1fdebb148ed23b51fd3c48e101f2a41b337341d31a2b61371699f352e07e"},{"excerpt":"[443e8bb18c3a] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Resumption must be refused whenever token-budget exhaustion interrupts mid-event, because the partial event either has no well-defined identity (cannot be referenced as a causal parent) or must be rewritten to become complete (violating append-only). There is no safe recovery path for mid-event interruption under strict append-only semantics.\nMECHANISM: An event in the log must be atomically committed: it has a beginning marker, content, an end marker, and a predecessor hash. If the token budget runs out between the beginning and end markers, the log contains a partial record. Any resumption faces a dilemma: (a) append a new event referencing the partial one—invalid because the partial event has no stable identity/hash; (b) rewrite the partial event to completion—violates append-only. Both paths corrupt identity, so refusal is the only responsible","handle":"A2","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"443e8bb18c3aeff752de3b1e1f418b024d40ac06354ed3ec954e1688c23b11b2"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact 443e8bb18c3aeff752de3b1e1f418b024d40ac06354ed3ec954e1688c23b11b2 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P2","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:b5ed3b96a7fa7980f83b1352d0b7103bcc60fd473b639a40a2cca04b962f525a"},{"excerpt":"[7b7a8f206495] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Append-only identity is never corrupted by resumption per se; corruption only occurs if the resumption re-evaluates or rewrites already-committed events. Therefore resumption is always permissible as long as the continuation starts strictly after the last committed event, regardless of whether the logical reasoning unit was complete.\nMECHANISM: Append-only is a storage invariant, not a semantic invariant. Each event is committed atomically with a content hash and predecessor link. The causal chain is a linked list; appending a new node after any committed node preserves the list's integrity. The logical incompleteness of the reasoning (e.g., a chain-of-thought cut mid-argument) is a semantic property orthogonal to the append-only storage identity. The resumed event simply declares itself as a continuation after event N, and the log remains valid.","handle":"A3","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"7b7a8f206495f67ed07749df99d3286ddf4797e45e7f3a283073f0645b0f8395"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact 7b7a8f206495f67ed07749df99d3286ddf4797e45e7f3a283073f0645b0f8395 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P3","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:f3442317c15280bebd9ac74a979c70e1703a7a78300e780388d3d8c668916ec1"},{"excerpt":"[02f430369393] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Resumption is safe only if the system emitted an explicit 'barrier' or 'suspend' event before or at the point of token-budget exhaustion. Without a barrier, even well-formed events may be part of an incomplete logical unit (e.g., a multi-event inference step), and resumption could produce events that semantically depend on context that was never written to the log.\nMECHANISM: A reasoning run produces events in logical units that may span multiple atomic events (e.g., 'begin inference step', 'intermediate result', 'commit inference step'). Token-budget exhaustion may interrupt after 'intermediate result' but before 'commit inference step'. The log now contains a dangling logical unit. Resumption would append 'commit inference step' but the causal context (working memory, attention state) that informed the original step is lost. A barrier event woul","handle":"A4","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"02f430369393fe957bfd5f81ce2a6cf341359f6c5132bd3e77a2b65a0e3e3578"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact 02f430369393fe957bfd5f81ce2a6cf341359f6c5132bd3e77a2b65a0e3e3578 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P4","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:d0d75a750873666e6726e0fd1f38510a0d75233ea898d4cfc50b04472bc203cb"},{"excerpt":"[86374c5b0008] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Resumption requires an exact causal fingerprint match: the triple (program_hash, input_hash, model_checkpoint_hash) at resumption must equal the triple at run start. Any divergence means resumed events belong to a different causal identity, making the append-only log internally inconsistent—a single log containing events from two causally distinct runs.\nMECHANISM: The identity of an event stream is not merely 'the same log file' but 'the same causal origin.' An event's identity is determined by the causal process that generated it: (program, inputs, model state). If any component changes (e.g., model weights updated, program patched, input modified), the resumed events are causally alien to the pre-interruption events. Inserting them into the same append-only log creates a Frankenstein stream: structurally append-only but causally incoherent. The ","handle":"A5","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"86374c5b0008573d439ce410030c57d86c5b6a3ddea680ff8b607e94f1321ebe"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact 86374c5b0008573d439ce410030c57d86c5b6a3ddea680ff8b607e94f1321ebe had formal status accepted.","handle":"P5","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:390243e35fbb8a46ecf380a1645d287b3c88896d2266583e71e276a9896a5a1d"},{"excerpt":"[87d12bb19f2b] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Adjudication between rival criteria: the 'last complete event' criterion and the 'causal fingerprint' criterion are both necessary but neither is sufficient alone. The correct criterion is their conjunction: resumption is safe iff (1) the last event is well-formed and complete, AND (2) the causal fingerprint matches, AND (3) no intermediate event declares logical incompleteness (e.g., a 'begin' without a matching 'commit'). This conjunction subsumes both rivals and adds a logical-unit integrity check.\nMECHANISM: The 'last complete event' criterion ensures structural integrity of the log. The 'causal fingerprint' criterion ensures causal identity continuity. The 'logical-unit integrity' criterion ensures semantic coherence: multi-event units must be complete or explicitly aborted. Token-budget exhaustion can violate any of the three independently: ","handle":"A6","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"87d12bb19f2ba65ca786dc0c6a45cba830eadbe1cc79f2369aa3bb4fdd1d8b61"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact 87d12bb19f2ba65ca786dc0c6a45cba830eadbe1cc79f2369aa3bb4fdd1d8b61 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P6","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:0d9e4434a158768cf748a8022735003e02a3771e3d9cc9917f77e3297a8f7266"},{"excerpt":"[032d4125e6b7] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: Resumption is permissible if and only if the system emitted an explicit barrier/suspend event prior to token-budget exhaustion, and the exact causal fingerprint (program, input, checkpoint) matches at resumption; otherwise, it must be refused.\nMECHANISM: Token-budget exhaustion is an asynchronous, non-logical interrupt. If it occurs mid-event, the partial event lacks a well-defined identity and must be discarded to prevent log corruption. If an explicit barrier was emitted, the event log up to that point is complete and well-defined. Resuming from this barrier requires an exact match of the causal fingerprint to guarantee deterministic continuation, preserving the append-only identity of subsequent events.","handle":"A7","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"032d4125e6b7718672a0ccaeb0b97a669e9f311755b54dee0b235210851451d5"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact 032d4125e6b7718672a0ccaeb0b97a669e9f311755b54dee0b235210851451d5 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P7","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:9cbc6504ee1f92bd772908b20f203e284f14692db9901ac858a11d1ac1ed2bc0"},{"excerpt":"[b0a3d571038c] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: Resumption must always be refused after token-budget exhaustion; only a fresh re-execution from the last globally committed event is valid, effectively treating the exhausted run as a failed transaction.\nMECHANISM: Because token-budget exhaustion is an environment-level truncation, it cannot be reliably mapped to a logical suspend event in the reasoning trace. Any attempt to resume implicitly injects an assumption about the exact state at exhaustion, violating the deterministic causal chain required by the append-only log. The only way to preserve event identity is to roll back to the last committed state and start a new bounded run with a new identity.","handle":"A8","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"b0a3d571038c9314c15157a120d764f0c6da706958888a3f119a9a2d2f7c49df"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact b0a3d571038c9314c15157a120d764f0c6da706958888a3f119a9a2d2f7c49df had formal status accepted.","handle":"P8","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:370e095599747da7455b8e03f6e99e5042513f04a39b9ab7e3b5c88f1458dedf"},{"excerpt":"[11082a195824] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: Append-only identity is preserved by treating resumption as a new derived branch rather than a continuation, making resumption always possible but strictly as a 'new' run.\nMECHANISM: When token-budget exhaustion occurs, the original run halts. To resume, the system emits a new 'BranchInit' event referencing the last known good event of the original run. This establishes a new causal fingerprint. The identity of the original events is preserved because they are never mutated or appended to, and the new events carry a distinct lineage, satisfying append-only constraints without needing a barrier.","handle":"A9","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"11082a195824e60914be82ed12caaad2160189e08cd5b0f62b7b6d7f3b44671e"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact 11082a195824e60914be82ed12caaad2160189e08cd5b0f62b7b6d7f3b44671e had formal status accepted.","handle":"P9","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:db1ac3d71ffabc64d9583b42fd6dcde0c643a1bf5f3590d39ee0e150fcc15978"},{"excerpt":"[b7174837d23f] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: Resumption is always safe at any point of exhaustion if the reasoning step is decomposed into idempotent, purely functional state transitions where token exhaustion only suspends the evaluation of the current step, not the commitment of the previous one.\nMECHANISM: In a purely functional event-sourced system, the log contains only fully applied state transitions. Token budget exhaustion halts the evaluation of the next transition, but since uncommitted transitions are never written, the log remains clean. Resumption simply re-attempts the evaluation of the current transition from scratch using the state of the last committed event. No explicit barrier is needed because partial states are inherently uncommitted and therefore non-existent in the append-only log.","handle":"A10","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"b7174837d23fe4c37e80cb3e27304ae050c0389249879df249e4658f3e79b6f3"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact b7174837d23fe4c37e80cb3e27304ae050c0389249879df249e4658f3e79b6f3 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P10","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:c9e5fc06b72b1b609efabc76496f015f380f177866266d256a996855d7785bae"},{"excerpt":"[a7eec3df4174] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: Resumption is safe iff the last committed entry that has full event framing (delimiters, checksum, causal fingerprint) is a complete event whose fingerprint matches the expected continuation point; any partial trailing fragment from mid-stream exhaustion is definitionally a non-event under append-only semantics and cannot corrupt event identity. Resumption must be refused when the last byte-stream boundary is ambiguous such that a partial fragment could be mistaken for a complete event.\nMECHANISM: Append-only identity attaches to complete, framed events, not to raw byte sequences. A partial fragment left by token-budget exhaustion lacks the closing delimiter and/or checksum that confer event status. The resumption protocol reads backward from the end of the log, verifies framing integrity, and quarantines (ignores) any trailing non-framed bytes. B","handle":"A11","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"a7eec3df417441c25086b4a1527cf8b0f24811157c0efaa0994b88278b404708"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact a7eec3df417441c25086b4a1527cf8b0f24811157c0efaa0994b88278b404708 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P11","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:09136f6b9f8d5171278b5c8279b995d92cdf55fafbc7a02623a37f94d36b5989"},{"excerpt":"[7bd092280603] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: A synthetic barrier/suspend event injected before predictable token-budget exhaustion satisfies the causal fingerprint requirement and makes resumption safe, resolving SRC_002's internal inconsistency: the categorical refusal is wrong because its own acknowledged countercondition (budget-exhaustion warning) is precisely the trigger for safe barrier injection.\nMECHANISM: When the reasoning envelope detects approaching budget exhaustion (e.g., 90% threshold), it emits a synthetic SuspendEvent with a causal fingerprint capturing the full continuation state. This event is complete and framed before the budget is fully consumed. On resumption, the protocol verifies the SuspendEvent's fingerprint and continues. The append-only log contains only complete events: the prior reasoning events plus the SuspendEvent. No partial fragment exists because the enve","handle":"A12","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"7bd092280603ad1e10ac0f2b1f78a4f913cf118a99ae9e878d256d3f06303bb8"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact 7bd092280603ad1e10ac0f2b1f78a4f913cf118a99ae9e878d256d3f06303bb8 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P12","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:a12224194c758c4753462cfca841ecc914866fed4b4eb42ea0a27425c41ee57c"},{"excerpt":"[ef7da8771375] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: The covering principle is: append-only identity is preserved on resumption whenever the protocol can deterministically quarantine the trailing partial fragment as a non-event, regardless of whether a barrier was explicitly emitted. The false dichotomy in SRC_001 (either explicit barrier or no safe recovery) is dissolved because a barrier is sufficient but not necessary—framing-based quarantine is the necessary and sufficient condition.\nMECHANISM: The distinction between 'last event written' and 'last byte written' is the key. Append-only identity is a property of events, not bytes. A barrier event makes quarantine trivial (the last event is explicitly marked as a suspend point). But framing-based quarantine achieves the same outcome: scan the log tail, identify the last byte sequence that satisfies complete-event framing criteria, treat everything","handle":"A13","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"ef7da8771375ef7d80430784ad56bdc653fdcddf87df063fa846db8b18598552"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact ef7da8771375ef7d80430784ad56bdc653fdcddf87df063fa846db8b18598552 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P13","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:8af4b9489567ef66e34ad87f5dabb5c588151d768e7835c98029a8872c9be4fc"},{"excerpt":"[87ab9c998956] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: Resumption must be refused not because of a partial fragment in the log (which is inert), but when and only when the causal fingerprint of the last complete event reveals that an intermediate consumer has already advanced derived state based on a speculative or partial read. The corruption risk lives in downstream state projections, not in the append-only log itself.\nMECHANISM: The append-only log is immutable by definition; a partial fragment cannot corrupt it because it was never granted event identity. The real risk is that some consumer read the partial fragment, interpreted it as a complete event, and updated a state projection. On resumption, the reasoning engine would replay from the last complete event and produce different events, creating a divergence between the log and the already-consumed projection. The causal fingerprint check detec","handle":"A14","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"87ab9c9989566c019d9423137554808b930b35cc1043dbfbd81974a4e70aaca7"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact 87ab9c9989566c019d9423137554808b930b35cc1043dbfbd81974a4e70aaca7 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P14","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:808870bd730d6fcc31aa71ad2229db1903c669155c808c202736ba635c605517"},{"excerpt":"[88da24f09264] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: Resumption is categorically unsafe if and only if the append-only medium does not support atomic delimitation of events—i.e., if a single write operation can produce a byte sequence that is indistinguishable from a complete event. When the medium supports atomic per-event writes (all-or-nothing delivery), every entry in the log is either a complete event or nothing, so token-budget exhaustion simply leaves the log at the last complete event, and resumption is always safe given a matching causal fingerprint.\nMECHANISM: The root question is whether the write boundary is atomic. If the medium guarantees that each event is written atomically (either fully committed or not at all), then partial fragments are impossible by construction. Token-budget exhaustion interrupts the reasoning process between event emissions, not within one. The log contains zer","handle":"A15","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"88da24f09264c102445cf6913e692621de1ffaed76ef369ca6d6cbe0366cfe33"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact 88da24f09264c102445cf6913e692621de1ffaed76ef369ca6d6cbe0366cfe33 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P15","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:1df9567e381b028344c72b0ccc4ad93c439e1477fa2b2f5113601a7b8e8d82ab"},{"excerpt":"[cd1151d9d151] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: The rival criteria are unified by a two-level invariant: (L1) log-level append-only identity requires that no committed event is ever modified or deleted; (L2) stream-level causal identity requires that the resumption produces the same event sequence that would have been produced without interruption. Resumption is safe iff L1 holds trivially (partial fragments are non-events) AND L2 holds (causal fingerprint of last complete event matches the continuation point AND no exogenous event was interleaved). Refusal is required when L2 fails even if L1 holds, because the resumed stream would diverge from the canonical stream.\nMECHANISM: SRC_001 conflates L1 and L2: it treats barrier absence as an L1 violation, but barriers address L2 (marking the continuation point), not L1 (which is about immutability of written events). SRC_002's three conditions are ","handle":"A16","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"cd1151d9d1519238a49fdb9c990d669fd68226114b1cda0e2359810f4ea70231"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact cd1151d9d1519238a49fdb9c990d669fd68226114b1cda0e2359810f4ea70231 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P16","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:e6bd63c730dc2301b4f8b937c49646ae2fccd93aa4607aa63e0bfc3a3f532284"},{"excerpt":"[d32ceab3793c] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: Resumption is permissible exactly when the event-framing protocol can deterministically identify the last complete committed event before the truncation point, quarantining any trailing partial bytes as non-events; it must be refused when framing is non-self-delimiting or when integrity checks reveal corruption within a nominally complete event.\nMECHANISM: Under the two-level invariant decomposition (physical append-only integrity vs logical causal continuity), physical integrity is preserved by framing-quarantine: a partial byte sequence left by mid-stream exhaustion was never an event, so ignoring it violates no append-only commitment. Logical continuity is preserved by causal fingerprint matching: the resumed stream must produce the same observable sequence as an uninterrupted run would have past the last committed event. When the framing proto","handle":"A17","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"d32ceab3793ce6b75fbf1628f671b3f3b300488127d8294ebc76d56e8019dc60"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact d32ceab3793ce6b75fbf1628f671b3f3b300488127d8294ebc76d56e8019dc60 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P17","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:9b7edfbbee66706578e6d3999b4795c2f424dfd4587135a4c0ad74dd9e2ddc39"},{"excerpt":"[4f5cb9912abc] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: Resumption must be refused when token-budget exhaustion occurs inside a causally atomic reasoning step whose internal state was only partially flushed, because re-entering that step from the outside produces a different causal history than uninterrupted execution would have—even though no committed event was modified or deleted.\nMECHANISM: Some reasoning-envelope operations are causally atomic: they emit a single event that summarizes a multi-token internal computation. If exhaustion interrupts the internal computation before the summarizing event is emitted, the resumption protocol sees no event for that step. Restarting the step from scratch is physically append-only-safe (nothing was committed to delete), but logically it produces a different causal fingerprint because the recomputation may draw different random samples, encounter different cac","handle":"A18","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"4f5cb9912abcc469e861df1daaa58617f3ec9916ecf8d8b21f11afee38f1434e"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact 4f5cb9912abcc469e861df1daaa58617f3ec9916ecf8d8b21f11afee38f1434e had formal status accepted.","handle":"P18","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:d583599f63a17d95c4abe556edaf10821c7df631ed2fad7f3a0e1a77724df6a7"},{"excerpt":"[ad7eb30cd2aa] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: Resumption is always permissible because the reasoning envelope proactively closes the event stream before budget exhaustion forces truncation, leaving no partial fragment; the only refusal condition is an implementation defect where the proactive-close mechanism fails to fire.\nMECHANISM: If the envelope monitors remaining budget and, upon reaching a pre-exhaustion threshold, flushes all pending state into a complete terminal event and closes the stream cleanly, then no partial fragment ever exists. The append-only log contains only complete events. Resumption is simply appending new events after the terminal close marker. The problem's demand for a refusal condition is satisfied by noting the single failure mode: the proactive-close mechanism itself fails (e.g., the budget monitor crashes before triggering close). In that case the log is in an in","handle":"A19","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"ad7eb30cd2aad6c6a7089084b8d3f7cb1b78ef48591107fec761feffeb09f083"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact ad7eb30cd2aad6c6a7089084b8d3f7cb1b78ef48591107fec761feffeb09f083 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P19","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:2dcdf0fc59546b3cbeee264bc0dd82c177ac6381c36570821692571a0e2ea830"},{"excerpt":"[0f27328131e4] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: Resumption is governed by case-matched evidence: the permissibility depends on which of four exhaustive cases the exhaustion falls into—(A) inter-event boundary, (B) intra-event but extra-checksum-verified, (C) intra-event with ambiguous framing, (D) intra-event with detected corruption—and only cases A and B permit resumption while C and D require refusal.\nMECHANISM: Anchoring in cases: Case A is the trivial safe case—the truncation falls on a clean record boundary, resumption appends next events, both invariants hold. Case B: truncation is intra-event but the event carries an end-of-record checksum that the resumption protocol can test; if the checksum-verified prefix is a complete event, quarantine the suffix and resume; if not, the record is partial and must be treated per case C. Case C: framing is ambiguous (length-prefix truncated, no check","handle":"A20","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"0f27328131e429230fa93f0428ee52e63b937c131ca7f8bf2b0da203d1a372f4"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact 0f27328131e429230fa93f0428ee52e63b937c131ca7f8bf2b0da203d1a372f4 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P20","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:60146cb9b72b61d0c805a1b03bc981c9ffda6120608229cdeb846d1b202500f8"},{"excerpt":"[86b43968adef] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: Resumption is permissible if and only if the resumption protocol can reconstruct the causal fingerprint from the committed log alone without reference to the partial fragment, because the partial fragment is causally inert; resumption must be refused when the partial fragment has leaked causal state into a side channel that the resumed stream would read.\nMECHANISM: The causal fingerprint is a function of the committed event sequence. A partial fragment, being a non-event, does not enter the fingerprint. Therefore resumption is safe whenever the resumed stream's observable output depends solely on committed events. However, if the reasoning envelope writes intermediate state to a side channel (e.g., a scratchpad, cache, or environment variable) that the resumed stream reads, then the partial fragment's side-channel residue is causally potent—it inf","handle":"A21","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"86b43968adeff3920380b4fa7f611d922e12396b498cdaf35a36dd40e065cfdd"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact 86b43968adeff3920380b4fa7f611d922e12396b498cdaf35a36dd40e065cfdd had formal status accepted.","handle":"P21","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:715c1045a675cdd20433c2e947ee4ea1a14ff52a77ff38634267d49cd0c36d94"},{"excerpt":"[b7841c980abc] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: Resumption criteria cannot be adjudicated from the available evidence because the reasoning-envelope-wf specification does not specify whether the event framing protocol is self-delimiting; the entire question reduces to this unresolved property, and without it both 'always permissible' and 'sometimes refuse' are underdetermined.\nMECHANISM: The problem asks to adjudicate between rival criteria 'wherever the evidence allows.' But the key disambiguating fact—whether the framing protocol uses self-delimiting records with integrity checks (SCR_005's open question)—is not resolved by any source or scratch artifact. If framing is self-delimiting with checksums, the mechanist position holds (quarantine is deterministic). If framing is length-prefix-only, the skeptic position holds (truncation of a length field is ambiguous). The unifier's two-level decom","handle":"A22","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"b7841c980abced89f5c18171a083eed1707e1524f66cb0f3c02d7b1b942ff34c"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact b7841c980abced89f5c18171a083eed1707e1524f66cb0f3c02d7b1b942ff34c had formal status accepted.","handle":"P22","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:3b8ad4d83e4ef240399c4bf0550d87b4a9ca0cd6bc8d6795418bba96b376bc65"},{"excerpt":"[27a270e1a9d0] (school: school-0)\nABSTRACTION: The problem of safe resumption after token-budget exhaustion abstracts the five foundation artifacts into a single decision predicate R = (L1 ∧ L2), where L1 = no committed event was modified or deleted (trivially preserved by append-only framing) and L2 = the resumed stream produces the same event sequence as uninterrupted execution. Each SRC artifact is a partial specialization: SRC_001 contributes the barrier as a sufficient L2 mechanism, SRC_002 contributes downstream-projection divergence as an L2 failure mode, SRC_004 contributes causally-atomic-step interruption as another L2 failure mode, and SRC_005 contributes side-channel leakage as the general L2 failure mode that subsumes SRC_002 and SRC_004. The isolated problem inherits this unified predicate. REFUTED IF: the causal fingerprint cannot be decomposed into independently verifiabl","handle":"A23","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"27a270e1a9d01f0256e1033ca8d096bf2ed82f655ae2c23cd5f5659e7922aadc"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact 27a270e1a9d01f0256e1033ca8d096bf2ed82f655ae2c23cd5f5659e7922aadc had formal status accepted.","handle":"P23","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:2b81a0cd4c24c7a50691687044641e0c997fcabe68c65baecf47272df4d01627"},{"excerpt":"[52653dd4cd45] (school: school-0)\nREDUCTION: SRC_004's refusal condition—exhaustion inside a causally atomic reasoning step whose internal state was partially flushed—reduces to SRC_005's side-channel leakage condition. The 'partially flushed internal state' in SRC_004 is a specific instance of 'partial fragment has leaked causal state into a side channel' in SRC_005: the flush target (scratchpad, cache, attention state) IS the side channel. Thus SRC_004's independent refusal criterion is not novel; it is a constructive specialization of SRC_005's general principle. The isolated problem depends on recognizing this reduction to avoid double-counting failure modes. REFUTED IF: one can exhibit a case where a causally atomic step was interrupted (SRC_004 triggers) but no state leaked to any side channel the resumed stream would read (SRC_005 does not trigger), proving the conditions are logi","handle":"A24","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"52653dd4cd45eece6e9996c5d543a975bf858d8e66db29a2a7f7db30cc96f294"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact 52653dd4cd45eece6e9996c5d543a975bf858d8e66db29a2a7f7db30cc96f294 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P24","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:a6c96dee2dc0573ac3d9c9b818ce5d3a9e78bd8c2d74a2ba1eac8b7dc8a3dc4f"},{"excerpt":"[1de19c54e39e] (school: school-0)\nCONTRADICTION: SRC_001 claims resumption is unsafe without an explicit barrier because resumed events may depend on lost causal context (working memory, attention state). This contradicts SRC_005's claim that resumption is safe whenever the partial fragment is causally inert and the resumed stream depends solely on committed events. Specifically, if the reasoning envelope has no side channels (SRC_005 countercondition), then the 'lost causal context' SRC_001 worries about was never durable and never influences the resumed stream—so resumption is safe without a barrier. The isolated problem must adjudicate this contradiction: either barriers are always necessary (SRC_001) or only necessary when side channels exist (SRC_005). REFUTED IF: stateless reasoning is shown to be impossible in practice (every reasoning step intrinsically relies on non-committed in","handle":"A25","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"1de19c54e39e3135f9db3b07972a95d4b025bc0cc1d7362962bdd403c265328d"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact 1de19c54e39e3135f9db3b07972a95d4b025bc0cc1d7362962bdd403c265328d had formal status accepted.","handle":"P25","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:e2a31b4da6bc6e1ef4ccb880d9cee7259f6ec92734efd9f647f63c6724b79716"},{"excerpt":"[f31d6660128c] (school: school-0)\nSHARED MECHANISM: SRC_002 and SRC_005 share the mechanism of causal-fingerprint-based divergence detection, but apply it to different corruption loci. SRC_002 detects divergence in downstream consumer state projections (a consumer read the partial fragment and advanced derived state). SRC_005 detects divergence via side-channel leakage (the partial fragment's residue influences resumed computation). Both share mechanism: the causal fingerprint must encode enough information to detect that the resumption point is causally consistent with the committed log. The isolated problem depends on this shared mechanism because both failure modes require the same fingerprint infrastructure to diagnose. REFUTED IF: the causal fingerprint encodes only event identities and not consumer acknowledgments (SRC_002 countercondition), making the two mechanisms operate on dis","handle":"A26","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"f31d6660128c1ec936af55e0c921d58ac586218770bc1d4834be4e48e37fc279"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 939, artifact f31d6660128c1ec936af55e0c921d58ac586218770bc1d4834be4e48e37fc279 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P26","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:7cfc7a48e362b03d09ac37f5c28a7d6aebed606b9b7832842c2d367d91f2d7ad"}],"formal_seq":939,"id":"sha256:f513bc79a325692c851996854ac366d550f98fb8e7118db970b322327f6bb407","legacy_citable_ids":["d1c3422e3d18","443e8bb18c3a","7b7a8f206495","02f430369393","86374c5b0008","87d12bb19f2b","032d4125e6b7","b0a3d571038c","11082a195824","b7174837d23f","a7eec3df4174","7bd092280603","ef7da8771375","87ab9c998956","88da24f09264","cd1151d9d151","d32ceab3793c","4f5cb9912abc","ad7eb30cd2aa","0f27328131e4","86b43968adef","b7841c980abc","27a270e1a9d0","52653dd4cd45","1de19c54e39e","f31d6660128c"],"legacy_text":"PROBLEM question-2da717017468d2da3c501dd1dc4ce3cd: Deep pass two: a bounded reasoning run stops on token-budget exhaustion. Determine exactly when it may later be resumed without corrupting the append-only identity of the recorded events, and when resumption must stay refused; adjudicate between rival criteria wherever the evidence allows.\n(family: 130 problems including spawned successors)\n\n== SURVIVING POSITIONS (accepted after criticism) ==\n[d1c3422e3d18] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Resumption is safe if and only if the last recorded event in the log is well-formed and complete, and the causal preconditions (program, inputs, model-state checkpoint) are demonstrably unchanged at resumption time. The append-only identity is preserved because new events extend the same causal chain without rewriting any prior event.\nMECHANISM: An event log maintains append-only identity through a causal chain: each event references its predecessor. Token-budget exhaustion is an external interrupt, not a logical termination. If the interrupt fell between complete events (not mid-write), the last event's hash serves as a resume cursor. The resumption process reads this cursor, verifies the program/input/model fingerprint matches, and appends new events that reference the last committed event as their causal parent. No rewrite occurs, so append-onl\n[443e8bb18c3a] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Resumption must be refused whenever token-budget exhaustion interrupts mid-event, because the partial event either has no well-defined identity (cannot be referenced as a causal parent) or must be rewritten to become complete (violating append-only). There is no safe recovery path for mid-event interruption under strict append-only semantics.\nMECHANISM: An event in the log must be atomically committed: it has a beginning marker, content, an end marker, and a predecessor hash. If the token budget runs out between the beginning and end markers, the log contains a partial record. Any resumption faces a dilemma: (a) append a new event referencing the partial one—invalid because the partial event has no stable identity/hash; (b) rewrite the partial event to completion—violates append-only. Both paths corrupt identity, so refusal is the only responsible\n[7b7a8f206495] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Append-only identity is never corrupted by resumption per se; corruption only occurs if the resumption re-evaluates or rewrites already-committed events. Therefore resumption is always permissible as long as the continuation starts strictly after the last committed event, regardless of whether the logical reasoning unit was complete.\nMECHANISM: Append-only is a storage invariant, not a semantic invariant. Each event is committed atomically with a content hash and predecessor link. The causal chain is a linked list; appending a new node after any committed node preserves the list's integrity. The logical incompleteness of the reasoning (e.g., a chain-of-thought cut mid-argument) is a semantic property orthogonal to the append-only storage identity. The resumed event simply declares itself as a continuation after event N, and the log remains valid.\n[02f430369393] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Resumption is safe only if the system emitted an explicit 'barrier' or 'suspend' event before or at the point of token-budget exhaustion. Without a barrier, even well-formed events may be part of an incomplete logical unit (e.g., a multi-event inference step), and resumption could produce events that semantically depend on context that was never written to the log.\nMECHANISM: A reasoning run produces events in logical units that may span multiple atomic events (e.g., 'begin inference step', 'intermediate result', 'commit inference step'). Token-budget exhaustion may interrupt after 'intermediate result' but before 'commit inference step'. The log now contains a dangling logical unit. Resumption would append 'commit inference step' but the causal context (working memory, attention state) that informed the original step is lost. A barrier event woul\n[86374c5b0008] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Resumption requires an exact causal fingerprint match: the triple (program_hash, input_hash, model_checkpoint_hash) at resumption must equal the triple at run start. Any divergence means resumed events belong to a different causal identity, making the append-only log internally inconsistent—a single log containing events from two causally distinct runs.\nMECHANISM: The identity of an event stream is not merely 'the same log file' but 'the same causal origin.' An event's identity is determined by the causal process that generated it: (program, inputs, model state). If any component changes (e.g., model weights updated, program patched, input modified), the resumed events are causally alien to the pre-interruption events. Inserting them into the same append-only log creates a Frankenstein stream: structurally append-only but causally incoherent. The \n[87d12bb19f2b] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Adjudication between rival criteria: the 'last complete event' criterion and the 'causal fingerprint' criterion are both necessary but neither is sufficient alone. The correct criterion is their conjunction: resumption is safe iff (1) the last event is well-formed and complete, AND (2) the causal fingerprint matches, AND (3) no intermediate event declares logical incompleteness (e.g., a 'begin' without a matching 'commit'). This conjunction subsumes both rivals and adds a logical-unit integrity check.\nMECHANISM: The 'last complete event' criterion ensures structural integrity of the log. The 'causal fingerprint' criterion ensures causal identity continuity. The 'logical-unit integrity' criterion ensures semantic coherence: multi-event units must be complete or explicitly aborted. Token-budget exhaustion can violate any of the three independently: \n[032d4125e6b7] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: Resumption is permissible if and only if the system emitted an explicit barrier/suspend event prior to token-budget exhaustion, and the exact causal fingerprint (program, input, checkpoint) matches at resumption; otherwise, it must be refused.\nMECHANISM: Token-budget exhaustion is an asynchronous, non-logical interrupt. If it occurs mid-event, the partial event lacks a well-defined identity and must be discarded to prevent log corruption. If an explicit barrier was emitted, the event log up to that point is complete and well-defined. Resuming from this barrier requires an exact match of the causal fingerprint to guarantee deterministic continuation, preserving the append-only identity of subsequent events.\n[b0a3d571038c] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: Resumption must always be refused after token-budget exhaustion; only a fresh re-execution from the last globally committed event is valid, effectively treating the exhausted run as a failed transaction.\nMECHANISM: Because token-budget exhaustion is an environment-level truncation, it cannot be reliably mapped to a logical suspend event in the reasoning trace. Any attempt to resume implicitly injects an assumption about the exact state at exhaustion, violating the deterministic causal chain required by the append-only log. The only way to preserve event identity is to roll back to the last committed state and start a new bounded run with a new identity.\n[11082a195824] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: Append-only identity is preserved by treating resumption as a new derived branch rather than a continuation, making resumption always possible but strictly as a 'new' run.\nMECHANISM: When token-budget exhaustion occurs, the original run halts. To resume, the system emits a new 'BranchInit' event referencing the last known good event of the original run. This establishes a new causal fingerprint. The identity of the original events is preserved because they are never mutated or appended to, and the new events carry a distinct lineage, satisfying append-only constraints without needing a barrier.\n[b7174837d23f] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: Resumption is always safe at any point of exhaustion if the reasoning step is decomposed into idempotent, purely functional state transitions where token exhaustion only suspends the evaluation of the current step, not the commitment of the previous one.\nMECHANISM: In a purely functional event-sourced system, the log contains only fully applied state transitions. Token budget exhaustion halts the evaluation of the next transition, but since uncommitted transitions are never written, the log remains clean. Resumption simply re-attempts the evaluation of the current transition from scratch using the state of the last committed event. No explicit barrier is needed because partial states are inherently uncommitted and therefore non-existent in the append-only log.\n[a7eec3df4174] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: Resumption is safe iff the last committed entry that has full event framing (delimiters, checksum, causal fingerprint) is a complete event whose fingerprint matches the expected continuation point; any partial trailing fragment from mid-stream exhaustion is definitionally a non-event under append-only semantics and cannot corrupt event identity. Resumption must be refused when the last byte-stream boundary is ambiguous such that a partial fragment could be mistaken for a complete event.\nMECHANISM: Append-only identity attaches to complete, framed events, not to raw byte sequences. A partial fragment left by token-budget exhaustion lacks the closing delimiter and/or checksum that confer event status. The resumption protocol reads backward from the end of the log, verifies framing integrity, and quarantines (ignores) any trailing non-framed bytes. B\n[7bd092280603] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: A synthetic barrier/suspend event injected before predictable token-budget exhaustion satisfies the causal fingerprint requirement and makes resumption safe, resolving SRC_002's internal inconsistency: the categorical refusal is wrong because its own acknowledged countercondition (budget-exhaustion warning) is precisely the trigger for safe barrier injection.\nMECHANISM: When the reasoning envelope detects approaching budget exhaustion (e.g., 90% threshold), it emits a synthetic SuspendEvent with a causal fingerprint capturing the full continuation state. This event is complete and framed before the budget is fully consumed. On resumption, the protocol verifies the SuspendEvent's fingerprint and continues. The append-only log contains only complete events: the prior reasoning events plus the SuspendEvent. No partial fragment exists because the enve\n[ef7da8771375] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: The covering principle is: append-only identity is preserved on resumption whenever the protocol can deterministically quarantine the trailing partial fragment as a non-event, regardless of whether a barrier was explicitly emitted. The false dichotomy in SRC_001 (either explicit barrier or no safe recovery) is dissolved because a barrier is sufficient but not necessary—framing-based quarantine is the necessary and sufficient condition.\nMECHANISM: The distinction between 'last event written' and 'last byte written' is the key. Append-only identity is a property of events, not bytes. A barrier event makes quarantine trivial (the last event is explicitly marked as a suspend point). But framing-based quarantine achieves the same outcome: scan the log tail, identify the last byte sequence that satisfies complete-event framing criteria, treat everything\n[87ab9c998956] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: Resumption must be refused not because of a partial fragment in the log (which is inert), but when and only when the causal fingerprint of the last complete event reveals that an intermediate consumer has already advanced derived state based on a speculative or partial read. The corruption risk lives in downstream state projections, not in the append-only log itself.\nMECHANISM: The append-only log is immutable by definition; a partial fragment cannot corrupt it because it was never granted event identity. The real risk is that some consumer read the partial fragment, interpreted it as a complete event, and updated a state projection. On resumption, the reasoning engine would replay from the last complete event and produce different events, creating a divergence between the log and the already-consumed projection. The causal fingerprint check detec\n[88da24f09264] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: Resumption is categorically unsafe if and only if the append-only medium does not support atomic delimitation of events—i.e., if a single write operation can produce a byte sequence that is indistinguishable from a complete event. When the medium supports atomic per-event writes (all-or-nothing delivery), every entry in the log is either a complete event or nothing, so token-budget exhaustion simply leaves the log at the last complete event, and resumption is always safe given a matching causal fingerprint.\nMECHANISM: The root question is whether the write boundary is atomic. If the medium guarantees that each event is written atomically (either fully committed or not at all), then partial fragments are impossible by construction. Token-budget exhaustion interrupts the reasoning process between event emissions, not within one. The log contains zer\n[cd1151d9d151] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: The rival criteria are unified by a two-level invariant: (L1) log-level append-only identity requires that no committed event is ever modified or deleted; (L2) stream-level causal identity requires that the resumption produces the same event sequence that would have been produced without interruption. Resumption is safe iff L1 holds trivially (partial fragments are non-events) AND L2 holds (causal fingerprint of last complete event matches the continuation point AND no exogenous event was interleaved). Refusal is required when L2 fails even if L1 holds, because the resumed stream would diverge from the canonical stream.\nMECHANISM: SRC_001 conflates L1 and L2: it treats barrier absence as an L1 violation, but barriers address L2 (marking the continuation point), not L1 (which is about immutability of written events). SRC_002's three conditions are \n[d32ceab3793c] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: Resumption is permissible exactly when the event-framing protocol can deterministically identify the last complete committed event before the truncation point, quarantining any trailing partial bytes as non-events; it must be refused when framing is non-self-delimiting or when integrity checks reveal corruption within a nominally complete event.\nMECHANISM: Under the two-level invariant decomposition (physical append-only integrity vs logical causal continuity), physical integrity is preserved by framing-quarantine: a partial byte sequence left by mid-stream exhaustion was never an event, so ignoring it violates no append-only commitment. Logical continuity is preserved by causal fingerprint matching: the resumed stream must produce the same observable sequence as an uninterrupted run would have past the last committed event. When the framing proto\n[4f5cb9912abc] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: Resumption must be refused when token-budget exhaustion occurs inside a causally atomic reasoning step whose internal state was only partially flushed, because re-entering that step from the outside produces a different causal history than uninterrupted execution would have—even though no committed event was modified or deleted.\nMECHANISM: Some reasoning-envelope operations are causally atomic: they emit a single event that summarizes a multi-token internal computation. If exhaustion interrupts the internal computation before the summarizing event is emitted, the resumption protocol sees no event for that step. Restarting the step from scratch is physically append-only-safe (nothing was committed to delete), but logically it produces a different causal fingerprint because the recomputation may draw different random samples, encounter different cac\n[ad7eb30cd2aa] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: Resumption is always permissible because the reasoning envelope proactively closes the event stream before budget exhaustion forces truncation, leaving no partial fragment; the only refusal condition is an implementation defect where the proactive-close mechanism fails to fire.\nMECHANISM: If the envelope monitors remaining budget and, upon reaching a pre-exhaustion threshold, flushes all pending state into a complete terminal event and closes the stream cleanly, then no partial fragment ever exists. The append-only log contains only complete events. Resumption is simply appending new events after the terminal close marker. The problem's demand for a refusal condition is satisfied by noting the single failure mode: the proactive-close mechanism itself fails (e.g., the budget monitor crashes before triggering close). In that case the log is in an in\n[0f27328131e4] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: Resumption is governed by case-matched evidence: the permissibility depends on which of four exhaustive cases the exhaustion falls into—(A) inter-event boundary, (B) intra-event but extra-checksum-verified, (C) intra-event with ambiguous framing, (D) intra-event with detected corruption—and only cases A and B permit resumption while C and D require refusal.\nMECHANISM: Anchoring in cases: Case A is the trivial safe case—the truncation falls on a clean record boundary, resumption appends next events, both invariants hold. Case B: truncation is intra-event but the event carries an end-of-record checksum that the resumption protocol can test; if the checksum-verified prefix is a complete event, quarantine the suffix and resume; if not, the record is partial and must be treated per case C. Case C: framing is ambiguous (length-prefix truncated, no check\n[86b43968adef] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: Resumption is permissible if and only if the resumption protocol can reconstruct the causal fingerprint from the committed log alone without reference to the partial fragment, because the partial fragment is causally inert; resumption must be refused when the partial fragment has leaked causal state into a side channel that the resumed stream would read.\nMECHANISM: The causal fingerprint is a function of the committed event sequence. A partial fragment, being a non-event, does not enter the fingerprint. Therefore resumption is safe whenever the resumed stream's observable output depends solely on committed events. However, if the reasoning envelope writes intermediate state to a side channel (e.g., a scratchpad, cache, or environment variable) that the resumed stream reads, then the partial fragment's side-channel residue is causally potent—it inf\n[b7841c980abc] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: Resumption criteria cannot be adjudicated from the available evidence because the reasoning-envelope-wf specification does not specify whether the event framing protocol is self-delimiting; the entire question reduces to this unresolved property, and without it both 'always permissible' and 'sometimes refuse' are underdetermined.\nMECHANISM: The problem asks to adjudicate between rival criteria 'wherever the evidence allows.' But the key disambiguating fact—whether the framing protocol uses self-delimiting records with integrity checks (SCR_005's open question)—is not resolved by any source or scratch artifact. If framing is self-delimiting with checksums, the mechanist position holds (quarantine is deterministic). If framing is length-prefix-only, the skeptic position holds (truncation of a length field is ambiguous). The unifier's two-level decom\n[27a270e1a9d0] (school: school-0)\nABSTRACTION: The problem of safe resumption after token-budget exhaustion abstracts the five foundation artifacts into a single decision predicate R = (L1 ∧ L2), where L1 = no committed event was modified or deleted (trivially preserved by append-only framing) and L2 = the resumed stream produces the same event sequence as uninterrupted execution. Each SRC artifact is a partial specialization: SRC_001 contributes the barrier as a sufficient L2 mechanism, SRC_002 contributes downstream-projection divergence as an L2 failure mode, SRC_004 contributes causally-atomic-step interruption as another L2 failure mode, and SRC_005 contributes side-channel leakage as the general L2 failure mode that subsumes SRC_002 and SRC_004. The isolated problem inherits this unified predicate. REFUTED IF: the causal fingerprint cannot be decomposed into independently verifiabl\n[52653dd4cd45] (school: school-0)\nREDUCTION: SRC_004's refusal condition—exhaustion inside a causally atomic reasoning step whose internal state was partially flushed—reduces to SRC_005's side-channel leakage condition. The 'partially flushed internal state' in SRC_004 is a specific instance of 'partial fragment has leaked causal state into a side channel' in SRC_005: the flush target (scratchpad, cache, attention state) IS the side channel. Thus SRC_004's independent refusal criterion is not novel; it is a constructive specialization of SRC_005's general principle. The isolated problem depends on recognizing this reduction to avoid double-counting failure modes. REFUTED IF: one can exhibit a case where a causally atomic step was interrupted (SRC_004 triggers) but no state leaked to any side channel the resumed stream would read (SRC_005 does not trigger), proving the conditions are logi\n[1de19c54e39e] (school: school-0)\nCONTRADICTION: SRC_001 claims resumption is unsafe without an explicit barrier because resumed events may depend on lost causal context (working memory, attention state). This contradicts SRC_005's claim that resumption is safe whenever the partial fragment is causally inert and the resumed stream depends solely on committed events. Specifically, if the reasoning envelope has no side channels (SRC_005 countercondition), then the 'lost causal context' SRC_001 worries about was never durable and never influences the resumed stream—so resumption is safe without a barrier. The isolated problem must adjudicate this contradiction: either barriers are always necessary (SRC_001) or only necessary when side channels exist (SRC_005). REFUTED IF: stateless reasoning is shown to be impossible in practice (every reasoning step intrinsically relies on non-committed in\n[f31d6660128c] (school: school-0)\nSHARED MECHANISM: SRC_002 and SRC_005 share the mechanism of causal-fingerprint-based divergence detection, but apply it to different corruption loci. SRC_002 detects divergence in downstream consumer state projections (a consumer read the partial fragment and advanced derived state). SRC_005 detects divergence via side-channel leakage (the partial fragment's residue influences resumed computation). Both share mechanism: the causal fingerprint must encode enough information to detect that the resumption point is causally consistent with the committed log. The isolated problem depends on this shared mechanism because both failure modes require the same fingerprint infrastructure to diagnose. REFUTED IF: the causal fingerprint encodes only event identities and not consumer acknowledgments (SRC_002 countercondition), making the two mechanisms operate on dis\n(+14 more omitted for budget)\n\n== PAIRWISE RULINGS ==\n\n== UNRESOLVED RIVALRIES (multiple survivors, undecided) ==\n(+2 more omitted for budget)\n\n== REFUTED POSITIONS (with the arguments that felled them) ==\n\n\nDIRECTIVE: from this record ONLY, produce the committed thesis (rules in your role brief). Cite bracketed ids exactly.","omissions":{"argued_refutations":0,"catalog_items":0,"open_rivals":2,"pairwise_rulings":0,"survivors":14},"open_rivals":[],"pairwise_rulings":[],"problem_family_refs":["question-2da717017468d2da3c501dd1dc4ce3cd","disc:question-2da717017468d2da3c501dd1dc4ce3cd","conn:d1c3422e3d18","conn:443e8bb18c3a","conn:7b7a8f206495","conn:02f430369393","conn:86374c5b0008","conn:87d12bb19f2b","conn:032d4125e6b7","conn:b0a3d571038c","conn:11082a195824","conn:b7174837d23f","conn:a7eec3df4174","conn:7bd092280603","conn:ef7da8771375","conn:87ab9c998956","conn:88da24f09264","conn:cd1151d9d151","conn:d32ceab3793c","conn:4f5cb9912abc","conn:ad7eb30cd2aa","conn:0f27328131e4","conn:86b43968adef","conn:b7841c980abc","research:reason-counter@aac4b2965c6b1faac6e52c8e:d1c3422e3d18","research:reason-counter@c0da307cbd6f85516b5af294:d1c3422e3d18","research:reason-counter@d418dfc981234caa861f76f6:d1c3422e3d18","research:reason-counter@3a45dd9c506dfc8fee216947:443e8bb18c3a","research:reason-counter@66c21c002b2336b26ded27ba:443e8bb18c3a","research:reason-counter@cec199643153e2c76372f78b:7b7a8f206495","research:reason-counter@3839d9b07e730501ac0c090c:7b7a8f206495","research:reason-counter@89ac589c8c3850c20a674d9e:7b7a8f206495","research:reason-counter@3c41a87bbb635a964ebb0f00:02f430369393","research:reason-counter@55309b9a81206a4ccb2bdfdb:02f430369393","research:reason-counter@deed7ad51aff067e807d9942:86374c5b0008","research:reason-counter@b25b5ffa8e2969d5fe081382