 school-0)\nCLAIM: Resumption is safe if and only if the last recorded event in the log is well-formed and complete, and the causal preconditions (program, inputs, model-state checkpoint) are demonstrably unchanged at resumption time. The append-only identity is preserved because new events extend the same causal chain without rewriting any prior event.\nMECHANISM: An event log maintains append-only identity through a causal chain: each event references its predecessor. Token-budget exhaustion is an external interrupt, not a logical termination. If the interrupt fell between complete events (not mid-write), the last event's hash serves as a resume cursor. The resumption process reads this cursor, verifies the program/input/model fingerprint matches, and appends new events that reference the last committed event as their causal parent. No rewrite occurs, so append-onl","school":"school-0"},{"addressed_problem_refs":["question-2da717017468d2da3c501dd1dc4ce3cd"],"artifact_ref":"443e8bb18c3aeff752de3b1e1f418b024d40ac06354ed3ec954e1688c23b11b2","citation_id":"443e8bb18c3a","claim":"Resumption must be refused whenever token-budget exhaustion interrupts mid-event, because the partial event either has no well-defined identity (cannot be referenced as a causal parent) or must be rewritten to become complete (violating append-only). There is no safe recovery path for mid-event interruption under strict append-only semantics.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"An event in the log must be atomically committed: it has a beginning marker, content, an end marker, and a predecessor hash. If the token budget runs out between the beginning and end markers, the log contains a partial record. Any resumption faces a dilemma: (a) append a new event referencing the partial one—invalid because the partial event has no stable identity/hash; (b) rewrite the partial event to completion—violates append-only. Both paths corrupt identity, so refusal is the only responsible action.","overturn_conditions":[],"rendered_text":"[443e8bb18c3a] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Resumption must be refused whenever token-budget exhaustion interrupts mid-event, because the partial event either has no well-defined identity (cannot be referenced as a causal parent) or must be rewritten to become complete (violating append-only). There is no safe recovery path for mid-event interruption under strict append-only semantics.\nMECHANISM: An event in the log must be atomically committed: it has a beginning marker, content, an end marker, and a predecessor hash. If the token budget runs out between the beginning and end markers, the log contains a partial record. Any resumption faces a dilemma: (a) append a new event referencing the partial one—invalid because the partial event has no stable identity/hash; (b) rewrite the partial event to completion—violates append-only. Both paths corrupt identity, so refusal is the only responsible","school":"school-0"},{"addressed_problem_refs":["question-2da717017468d2da3c501dd1dc4ce3cd"],"artifact_ref":"7b7a8f206495f67ed07749df99d3286ddf4797e45e7f3a283073f0645b0f8395","citation_id":"7b7a8f206495","claim":"Append-only identity is never corrupted by resumption per se; corruption only occurs if the resumption re-evaluates or rewrites already-committed events. Therefore resumption is always permissible as long as the continuation starts strictly after the last committed event, regardless of whether the logical reasoning unit was complete.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"Append-only is a storage invariant, not a semantic invariant. Each event is committed atomically with a content hash and predecessor link. The causal chain is a linked list; appending a new node after any committed node preserves the list's integrity. The logical incompleteness of the reasoning (e.g., a chain-of-thought cut mid-argument) is a semantic property orthogonal to the append-only storage identity. The resumed event simply declares itself as a continuation after event N, and the log remains valid.","overturn_conditions":[],"rendered_text":"[7b7a8f206495] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Append-only identity is never corrupted by resumption per se; corruption only occurs if the resumption re-evaluates or rewrites already-committed events. Therefore resumption is always permissible as long as the continuation starts strictly after the last committed event, regardless of whether the logical reasoning unit was complete.\nMECHANISM: Append-only is a storage invariant, not a semantic invariant. Each event is committed atomically with a content hash and predecessor link. The causal chain is a linked list; appending a new node after any committed node preserves the list's integrity. The logical incompleteness of the reasoning (e.g., a chain-of-thought cut mid-argument) is a semantic property orthogonal to the append-only storage identity. The resumed event simply declares itself as a continuation after event N, and the log remains valid.","school":"school-0"},{"addressed_problem_refs":["question-2da717017468d2da3c501dd1dc4ce3cd"],"artifact_ref":"02f430369393fe957bfd5f81ce2a6cf341359f6c5132bd3e77a2b65a0e3e3578","citation_id":"02f430369393","claim":"Resumption is safe only if the system emitted an explicit 'barrier' or 'suspend' event before or at the point of token-budget exhaustion. Without a barrier, even well-formed events may be part of an incomplete logical unit (e.g., a multi-event inference step), and resumption could produce events that semantically depend on context that was never written to the log.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"A reasoning run produces events in logical units that may span multiple atomic events (e.g., 'begin inference step', 'intermediate result', 'commit inference step'). Token-budget exhaustion may interrupt after 'intermediate result' but before 'commit inference step'. The log now contains a dangling logical unit. Resumption would append 'commit inference step' but the causal context (working memory, attention state) that informed the original step is lost. A barrier event would have captured this context. Without it, the resumed events are causally disconnected from the reasoning state that produced the prior events, corrupting identity.","overturn_conditions":[],"rendered_text":"[02f430369393] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Resumption is safe only if the system emitted an explicit 'barrier' or 'suspend' event before or at the point of token-budget exhaustion. Without a barrier, even well-formed events may be part of an incomplete logical unit (e.g., a multi-event inference step), and resumption could produce events that semantically depend on context that was never written to the log.\nMECHANISM: A reasoning run produces events in logical units that may span multiple atomic events (e.g., 'begin inference step', 'intermediate result', 'commit inference step'). Token-budget exhaustion may interrupt after 'intermediate result' but before 'commit inference step'. The log now contains a dangling logical unit. Resumption would append 'commit inference step' but the causal context (working memory, attention state) that informed the original step is lost. A barrier event woul","school":"school-0"},{"addressed_problem_refs":["question-2da717017468d2da3c501dd1dc4ce3cd"],"artifact_ref":"86374c5b0008573d439ce410030c57d86c5b6a3ddea680ff8b607e94f1321ebe","citation_id":"86374c5b0008","claim":"Resumption requires an exact causal fin