chool-1)\nCLAIM: Resumption is permissible if and only if the system emitted an explicit barrier/suspend event prior to token-budget exhaustion, and the exact causal fingerprint (program, input, checkpoint) matches at resumption; otherwise, it must be refused.\nMECHANISM: Token-budget exhaustion is an asynchronous, non-logical interrupt. If it occurs mid-event, the partial event lacks a well-defined identity and must be discarded to prevent log corruption. If an explicit barrier was emitted, the event log up to that point is complete and well-defined. Resuming from this barrier requires an exact match of the causal fingerprint to guarantee deterministic continuation, preserving the append-only identity of subsequent events.","school":"school-1"},{"addressed_problem_refs":["question-2da717017468d2da3c501dd1dc4ce3cd"],"artifact_ref":"b0a3d571038c9314c15157a120d764f0c6da706958888a3f119a9a2d2f7c49df","citation_id":"b0a3d571038c","claim":"Resumption must always be refused after token-budget exhaustion; only a fresh re-execution from the last globally committed event is valid, effectively treating the exhausted run as a failed transaction.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","443e8bb18c3aeff752de3b1e1f418b024d40ac06354ed3ec954e1688c23b11b2"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"Because token-budget exhaustion is an environment-level truncation, it cannot be reliably mapped to a logical suspend event in the reasoning trace. Any attempt to resume implicitly injects an assumption about the exact state at exhaustion, violating the deterministic causal chain required by the append-only log. The only way to preserve event identity is to roll back to the last committed state and start a new bounded run with a new identity.","overturn_conditions":[],"rendered_text":"[b0a3d571038c] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: Resumption must always be refused after token-budget exhaustion; only a fresh re-execution from the last globally committed event is valid, effectively treating the exhausted run as a failed transaction.\nMECHANISM: Because token-budget exhaustion is an environment-level truncation, it cannot be reliably mapped to a logical suspend event in the reasoning trace. Any attempt to resume implicitly injects an assumption about the exact state at exhaustion, violating the deterministic causal chain required by the append-only log. The only way to preserve event identity is to roll back to the last committed state and start a new bounded run with a new identity.","school":"school-1"},{"addressed_problem_refs":["question-2da717017468d2da3c501dd1dc4ce3cd"],"artifact_ref":"11082a195824e60914be82ed12caaad2160189e08cd5b0f62b7b6d7f3b44671e","citation_id":"11082a195824","claim":"Append-only identity is preserved by treating resumption as a new derived branch rather than a continuation, making resumption always possible but strictly as a 'new' run.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","d1c3422e3d18993ac034bc5bbd3b90f90d3294847aca130493c4c8c6272330cb"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"When token-budget exhaustion occurs, the original run halts. To resume, the system emits a new 'BranchInit' event referencing the last known good event of the original run. This establishes a new causal fingerprint. The identity of the original events is preserved because they are never mutated or appended to, and the new events carry a distinct lineage, satisfying append-only constraints without needing a barrier.","overturn_conditions":[],"rendered_text":"[11082a195824] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: Append-only identity is preserved by treating resumption as a new derived branch rather than a continuation, making resumption always possible but strictly as a 'new' run.\nMECHANISM: When token-budget exhaustion occurs, the original run halts. To resume, the system emits a new 'BranchInit' event referencing the last known good event of the original run. This establishes a new causal fingerprint. The identity of the original events is preserved because they are never mutated or appended to, and the new events carry a distinct lineage, satisfying append-only constraints without needing a barrier.","school":"school-1"},{"addressed_problem_refs":["question-2da717017468d2da3c501dd1dc4ce3cd"],"artifact_ref":"b7174837d23fe4c37e80cb3e27304ae050c0389249879df249e4658f3e79b6f3","citation_id":"b7174837d23f","claim":"Resumption is always safe at any point of exhaustion if the reasoning step is decomposed into idempotent, purely functional state transitions where token exhaustion only suspends the evaluation of the current step, not the commitment of the previous one.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"In a purely functional event-sourced system, the log contains only fully applied state transitions. Token budget exhaustion halts the evaluation of the next transition, but since uncommitted transitions are never written, the log remains clean. Resumption simply re-attempts the evaluation of the current transition from scratch using the state of the last committed event. No explicit barrier is needed because partial states are inherently uncommitted and therefore non-existent in the append-only log.","overturn_conditions":[],"rendered_text":"[b7174837d23f] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: Resumption is always safe at any point of exhaustion if the reasoning step is decomposed into idempotent, purely functional state transitions where token exhaustion only suspends the evaluation of the current step, not the commitment of the previous one.\nMECHANISM: In a purely functional event-sourced system, the log contains only fully applied state transitions. Token budget exhaustion halts the evaluation of the next transition, but since uncommitted transitions are never written, the log remains clean. Resumption simply re-attempts the evaluation of the current transition from scratch using the state of the last committed event. No explicit barrier is needed because partial states are inherently uncommitted and therefore non-existent in the append-only log.","school":"school-1"},{"addressed_problem_refs":["question-2da717017468d2da3c501dd1dc4ce3cd"],"artifact_ref":"a7eec3df417441c25086b4a1527cf8b0f24811157c0efaa0994b88278b404708","citation_id":"a7eec3df4174","claim":"Resumption is safe iff the last committed entry that has full event framing (delimiters, checksum, causal fingerprint) is a complete event whose fingerprint matches the expected continuation point; any partial trailing fragment from mid-stream exhaustion is definitionally a non-event under append-only semantics and cannot corrupt event identity. Resumption must be refused when the last byte-stream boundary is ambiguous such that a partial fragment could be mistaken for a complete event.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","243a4fedc1c425aac5f92b24d39a36def0dc077575d2cb0cc2d1323f7bf8f6df","e0fe0342ddd25401d1489e8a051f8e72fd411c608b2e9adbb8e72a1076962ef8"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"Append-only identity attaches to complete, framed events, not to raw byte sequences. A partial fragment left by token-budget exhaustion lacks the closing delimiter and/or checksum that confer event status. The resumption protocol reads backward from the end of the log, verifies framing integrity, and quarantines (ignores) any trailing non-framed bytes. Because no event identity was ever assigned to those bytes, ignoring them does not modify, delete, or rewrite any recorded event—