her a barrier was explicitly emitted. The false dichotomy in SRC_001 (either explicit barrier or no safe recovery) is dissolved because a barrier is sufficient but not necessary—framing-based quarantine is the necessary and sufficient condition.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","ca022252a879b666978ebc0e62bdcbde122f30692d0c2c897d489b9d476b0998","e0fe0342ddd25401d1489e8a051f8e72fd411c608b2e9adbb8e72a1076962ef8","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"The distinction between 'last event written' and 'last byte written' is the key. Append-only identity is a property of events, not bytes. A barrier event makes quarantine trivial (the last event is explicitly marked as a suspend point). But framing-based quarantine achieves the same outcome: scan the log tail, identify the last byte sequence that satisfies complete-event framing criteria, treat everything after as inert bytes. The log is append-only in terms of events; the inert tail is not an event and appending after it (by overwriting or skipping) does not violate append-only semantics because no event existed there to corrupt.","overturn_conditions":[],"rendered_text":"[ef7da8771375] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: The covering principle is: append-only identity is preserved on resumption whenever the protocol can deterministically quarantine the trailing partial fragment as a non-event, regardless of whether a barrier was explicitly emitted. The false dichotomy in SRC_001 (either explicit barrier or no safe recovery) is dissolved because a barrier is sufficient but not necessary—framing-based quarantine is the necessary and sufficient condition.\nMECHANISM: The distinction between 'last event written' and 'last byte written' is the key. Append-only identity is a property of events, not bytes. A barrier event makes quarantine trivi