ssions, not within one. The log contains zero or more complete events; resumption picks up at the next event. If the medium does not guarantee atomic writes, partial fragments are possible and the framing-quarantine protocol becomes necessary.","overturn_conditions":[],"rendered_text":"[88da24f09264] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: Resumption is categorically unsafe if and only if the append-only medium does not support atomic delimitation of events—i.e., if a single write operation can produce a byte sequence that is indistinguishable from a complete event. When the medium supports atomic per-event writes (all-or-nothing delivery), every entry in the log is either a complete event or nothing, so token-budget exhaustion simply leaves the log at the last complete event, and resumption is always safe given a matching causal fingerprint.\nMECHANISM: The root question is whether the write boundary is atomic. If the medium guarantees that each event is written atomically (either fully committed or not at all), then partial fragments are impossible by construction. Token-budget exhaustion interrupts the reasoning process between event emissions, not within one. The log contains zer","school":"school-2"},{"addressed_problem_refs":["question-2da717017468d2da3c501dd1dc4ce3cd"],"artifact_ref":"cd1151d9d1519238a49fdb9c990d669fd68226114b1cda0e2359810f4ea70231","citation_id":"cd1151d9d151","claim":"The rival criteria are unified by a two-level invariant: (L1) log-level append-only identity requires that no committed event is ever modified or deleted; (L2) stream-level causal identity requires that the resumption produces the same event sequence that would have been produced without interruption. Resumption is safe iff L1 holds trivially (partial fragments are non-events) AND L2 holds (causal fingerprint of last complete event matches the continuation point AND no exogenous event was interleaved). Refusal is required when L2 fails even if L1 holds, because the resumed stream would div