ustion forces truncation, leaving no partial fragment; the only refusal condition is an implementation defect where the proactive-close mechanism fails to fire.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["8fcab9cf971fff84470018f7b4d3f8cdf933532136f1cbaaefcb6d3bd7fffee2","ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"If the envelope monitors remaining budget and, upon reaching a pre-exhaustion threshold, flushes all pending state into a complete terminal event and closes the stream cleanly, then no partial fragment ever exists. The append-only log contains only complete events. Resumption is simply appending new events after the terminal close marker. The problem's demand for a refusal condition is satisfied by noting the single failure mode: the proactive-close mechanism itself fails (e.g., the budget monitor crashes before triggering close). In that case the log is in an indeterminate state and resumption must be refused until an integrity scan confirms the last record is complete.","overturn_conditions":[],"rendered_text":"[ad7eb30cd2aa] (school: school-3)\nCLAIM: Resumption is always permissible because the reasoning envelope proactively closes the event stream before budget exhaustion forces truncation, leaving no partial fragment; the only refusal condition is an implementation defect where the proactive-close mechanism fails to fire.\nMECHANISM: If the envelope monitors remaining budget and, upon reaching a pre-exhaustion threshold, flushes all pending state into a complete terminal event and closes the stream cleanly, then no partial fragment ever exists. The append-only log contains only complete events. Resumption is simply appending new events after the terminal close marker. The problem's demand for a refusal condition is satisfied by noting the single failure mode: the proactive-close mechanism itself fails (e.g., the budget monitor crashes before triggering close). In that case the log is in an in","school":"school-3"},{"addressed_problem_refs":["question-2da717017468d2da3c501dd1dc4ce3cd"],"artifact_ref":"0f27328131e429230fa93f0428ee52e63b937c131ca7f8bf2b0da203d1a372f4","citation_id":"0f27328131e4","claim":"Resumption is governed by case-matched evidence: the permissibility depends on which of four exhaustive cases the exhaustion falls into—(A) inter-event boundary, (B) intra-event but extra-checksum-verified, (C) intra-event with ambiguous framing, (D) intra-event with detected corruption—and only cases A and B permit resumption while C and D require refusal.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"Anchoring in cases: Case A is the trivial safe case—the truncation falls on a clean record boundary, resumption appends next events, both invariants hold. Case B: truncation is intra-event but the event carries an end-of-record checksum that the resumption protocol can test; if the checksum-verified prefix is a complete event, quarantine the suffix and resume; if not, the record is partial and must be treated per case C. Case C: framing is ambiguous (length-prefix truncated, no checksum), so the protocol cannot deterministically decide whether the last bytes constitute a partial or complete event—resumption would risk logical corruption, so refuse. Case D: integrity checks reveal corruption within what framing marks as a complete event—physical append-only identity itself is threatened, so refuse and require audit. This four-case partition adjudicates between the mechanist (who collapses to 'always A') and the skeptic (who collapses to 'always C/D').","ov